{"text": "BROWN & WILLIAMSON TOBACCO CORPORATION\nRESEARCH & DEVELOPMENT\n\nINTERNAL CORRESPONDENCE\n\nto: T..F. Riehl\nce: J. Le Knoop Ls Reed\nB. EB. Bacon S.C, Wilkerson\nPLR. Fisher R. G. Daniel\nRL R, Black\n\nFROM: \u2014R. Wilson\nDATE: September 24, 1987\n\n\u2018SUBJECT: LUCKY STRIKE CASED BURLEY STRIPS FOR CANARY ISLANDS/O31\n\nMacon has in excess of 180,000 pounds of subject tobacco rejected out of\nspecification.\n\nFor some time now, we have been replacing 10% of the GENERIC NM burley\nside with this tobacco and the reject rate is often more than the amount\nreintroduced into usage; therefore, the accumulated inventories are not\ndisappearing.\n\nRejection criteria operates on a very tight tolerance and in many\ninstances a box of packed cased strips is rejected because the net weight\nexceeds the tolerance limit; other frequent reasons for rejection are\n\u201cout of specification\" for moisture and/or particle size.\n\nT have discussed a proposal with P. R. Fisher and have his support for\nincreasing the rate of return in GENERIC NM to 20% maximus. The rate of\nreturn would \"float\" and be dependent upon the rate of rejection and to\navoid building inventories of the reject material.\n\nA 20% replacement on the burley side of the GENERIC NM blend (25% burley\nin the total blend) represents a 5% total blend inclusion.\n\nThe grade composition of the cased burley blend Canary Islands is\nsufficiently similar to the grade composition of the GENERIC NM burley\nblend component that a 20% level of inclusion would not be expected to\nalter the smoking quality of the finished product (see attached).\n\nTE you agree, we will proceed to implement this substitution.\n\nfas\n\nor\nMise\nAetachoent 621032885", "words": [{"text": "BROWN", "x0": 230, "y0": 100, "x1": 265, "y1": 109, "conf": 81.0}, {"text": "&", "x0": 272, "y0": 100, "x1": 279, "y1": 109, "conf": 91.0}, {"text": "WILLIAMSON", "x0": 286, "y0": 100, "x1": 356, "y1": 109, "conf": 58.0}, {"text": "TOBAC